17] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1045 "Pascal's Triangle and the Binomial Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172193 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5ec40baf-865b-5a44-991d-3e7fe88c0d58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=[]+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1afc9131-fb54-545a-abcd-cbf497ee2da8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172596 #edb/ref 1074 "To find $(\\frac{3}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{1})=(\\frac{2}{0})+(\\frac{2}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{0}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1061 18724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2865273f-2c72-526a-8ae4-5f9b2067a41a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1061 18731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1189fea7-65d2-5d2d-87d5-800d8204673c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172764 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1061 18977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "133af8ac-b2ce-5603-9334-ce3da1e8d068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1061 19269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b7576cb-372f-5a6a-ad5b-4f0b958a39e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1061 23007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2f96998-bbd7-586b-b0d8-4cdbe2c42034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (on the left) is position number $0.$\n\nWe can write a value in Pascal's triangle as $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}},$ where the value is located in row $\\color{red}a$ and position ${\\color{blue}b}.$\nThis is known as a **binomial coefficient**.\nIn words, we say $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}}$ as \"$\\color{red}{a}$ choose ${\\color{blue}b}$\".\n\nFor example, the value located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{{\\color{red}4} \\choose \\color{blue}{2}} = 6.$ Remember, we start counting from zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1065 3393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "62d4b142-a86a-52c9-b45a-1153afedf865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173144 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we wanted to calculate the value of $\\displaystyle{30 \\choose 3}.$ If we were to use Pascal's triangle, we'd have to write out $31$ rows, which would take a long time. Thankfully, there is a formula for calculating binomial coefficients. \n\nThe binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1065 3893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b89d1a7e-10c1-5ec2-b745-c3c45e0d7814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173146 #edb/ref 1067 "A Formula for the Binomial Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333061 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{1})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0764c515-8bca-5fd4-91d0-405b62507b8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333612 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{1})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333612 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333612 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42eec711-2366-5e9e-b394-97fc17f3a880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333660 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333660 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333660 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48cd114b-2976-5998-8aab-6731eae06237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333773 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{1})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333773 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333773 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56a6c0b8-3c50-57e0-a732-7b6b3ad0a73b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333852 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{1})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333852 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333852 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5e57c96a-894f-5a42-9888-8daa5fefe00b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334055 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{0})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334055 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334055 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "75f609f8-4a1f-5d10-a85e-5c13f336ab51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334486 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{0})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334486 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334486 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aace99ba-6af1-5dd7-8bf4-07b6ec0c5a0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334688 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{1}{0})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334688 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334688 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3e20e63-39e0-548b-908a-12e537bb3708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335029 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{0})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e959860f-3de8-5a3c-9c3a-cf7999bb9d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335106 #edb/ref 1080 "(\\frac{2}{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335106 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335106 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f192f51a-52e9-5d55-a6f8-773a74eea617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186335029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335310 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "25dbbbbc-4db9-57a7-8794-1b47f6589141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335645 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8ac1b6d-c8df-5a4f-8cb7-7837698f5128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{2})=(\\frac{2}{1})+[]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "911738be-601f-52ff-bdd9-bde88253aa9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335995 #edb/ref 1074 "To find $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{2})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{2})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{2})=(\\frac{2}{1})+(\\frac{2}{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
